The purpose of the code of ethics is to:
(1) Encourage high ethical standards in official conduct by city officials;
(2) Establish guidelines for ethical standards of conduct for all such officials by setting forth those acts or actions that are incompatible with the interests of the city;
(3) Require disclosure by such officials of private financial or other interest in matters affecting the city; and
(4) Serve as a basis for disciplining those who refuse to abide by its terms.
(Ord. No. 2010-04, § 1.1, 4-6-2010)

141:
(a) The proper government and administration of the city requires that:
(1) Its officials, employees, appointees, and volunteers conducting official city business, including those appointed by city council, as a group and as individuals subscribe to the following ethics and principles:
a. Serve others and not themselves;
b. Be independent, impartial and responsible;
c. Use resources with efficiency and economy;
d. Treat all people fairly;
e. Use the power of their position for the well-being of their constituents; and
f. Create an environment of honesty, openness and integrity;
(2) Governmental policies and decisions are made in the proper channels of the government structure;
(3) Public office and employment not to be used for undue personal gain; and
(4) The citizens of the city have confidence in the integrity of their government.
(b) Because the attainment of one or more of these ends is impaired whenever there exists in fact, or appears to exist, a conflict between the private interests and public responsibilities of city officials and employees, the public interest mandates that the city protect such conflicts of interest by establishing appropriate ethical standards with respect to the conduct of its officials and employees in situations in which a conflict may exist or appear to exist.
(c) Yet it is also essential to the efficient operation of the city that those persons best qualified be encouraged to serve in positions of public trust. Accordingly, the standards set forth in this article must be understood and interpreted in a way that will not unreasonably frustrate or impede the desire to seek public office by those best qualified to serve. To that end, officials and employees should not, except as otherwise provided by law, be denied the opportunity available to all citizens to acquire and maintain private, economic and other interests except when a conflict would necessarily result.
(d) The policy and purpose of this article, therefore, is to clarify those standards of ethical conduct that shall be applicable to the officials and employees of the city in the discharge of their public duties and to foster the development and maintenance of a tradition of responsible and effective public service.
(Ord. No. 2010-04, § 1.3, 4-6-2010)

148:
The constituted board of ethics shall have the following duties and powers:
(1) To administer the ethics ordinance;
(2) To receive, review, investigate, process, make determinations on, and hear complaints of violations of standards required by this article;
(3) To make such investigation and response to a complaint as it deems necessary to determine whether any person has violated any provisions of this article;
(4) To hold such hearing and make such inquiries as deemed necessary to investigate and rule upon complaints;
(5) To report its findings and recommended actions to the city council;
(6) All other duties and powers which may be listed in this article.
(Ord. No. 2010-04, § 2.2, 4-6-2010)

168:
(a) The city council may delegate the purchase of equipment, vehicles or other matters not required by state law to be done through competitive bidding. The delegation of purchases pursuant to this subsection shall require a city council vote approving such delegation, and shall only be allowed either to an employee or a council member. The employee or council member with delegated purchasing authority shall base purchasing decisions on the following considerations:
(1) Price;
(2) Availability and flexibility of the vendor on beginning and completion dates;
(3) Location of supplier, contractor or vendor with preference given to local bidders;
(4) A positive prior experience with the vendor by the city, including promptness of completing the work, responsiveness to change orders, repair and warranty work or other factors;
(5) A demonstration by the supplier, contractor or vendor to be environmentally friendly;
(6) Other such considerations as may be specified by the city council in the decision granting the delegation of authority.
(b) Nothing in this section shall limit the mayor's authority to approve contracts as set forth in section 2-250.
(Ord. No. 2022-04, ch. II, § 3, 9-13-2022)

170:
All purchases which are required to be made by competitive bid shall be made in accordance with state law. Considerations for awarding a bid shall be given to the following:
(1) Price;
(2) Availability and flexibility of the vendor on beginning and completion dates;
(3) The location of the supplier, contractor or vendor, with preference given to local bidders whenever possible and appropriate, as the city council deems hiring local vendors to be a stimulus to the local area and a support to the local economy. The city council deems that for a local vendor to be eligible to receive a preference, the vendor must have equal experience and capability to other bidders for the job being bid;
(4) Positive prior experience with the vendor by the city or with individual city council members, including promptness of completing the work, responsiveness to change orders, repair and warranty work or other factors;
(5) A demonstration by the supplier, contractor or vendor to be environmentally friendly;
(6) Other such considerations as may be specified by the city council in the bid materials.
(Ord. No. 2022-04, ch. II, § 5, 9-13-2022)

173:
(a) The city may sell, convey, transfer or otherwise dispose of surplus real property under such terms and conditions as the city council shall determine upon the approval of a resolution authorizing the sale and specifying the manner in which such sale is to be conducted. The sale of surplus real property shall be by quitclaim deed.
(b) In the event that the surplus property is a small or narrow strip or strips of land, so shaped or so small as to be incapable of being used independently as zoned or under applicable development regulations, or as streets, the city may sell, convey, transfer or otherwise dispose of the property to abutting property owners upon the following terms and conditions:
(1) The city council has approved a resolution declaring the property to be surplus and approving it for sale, conveyance, transfer or disposal under whatever terms and conditions set by the council;
(2) Each owner of property which abuts the surplus property has been notified in writing at least 14 days prior to the sale or conveyance and is afforded an opportunity to purchase the property under the terms and conditions set by the council;
(3) The sale of surplus real property shall be by quitclaim deed;
(4) The purchase price shall be payable in cash at closing; and
(5) The purchaser shall be responsible for payment of all costs associated with the purchase of the surplus property, including, but not limited to, any attorney's fees, surveyor's fees and recording fees in connection therewith.
(Ord. No. 2022-04, ch. II, § 8, 9-13-2022)

